    "### 3.1 Supervised Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "**Definition:** The algorithm is trained on a **labelled dataset** — each training example pairs an input with a known correct output. The model learns a mapping function from inputs to outputs and is then used to predict outputs for new inputs.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two main tasks:**\n",
    "- *Classification*: predict a discrete category (spam / not spam, approve / reject loan)\n",
    "- *Regression*: predict a continuous value (house price, stock return)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example scenario:** A bank trains a model on 10,000 past loan applications, each labelled as \"defaulted\" or \"repaid\". The model learns which combinations of income, credit history, and loan amount correlate with default, and then applies this knowledge to new applicants.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3.2 Unsupervised Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "**Definition:** The algorithm is given data **without any labels** and must discover inherent structure, patterns, or groupings on its own.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two main tasks:**\n",
    "- *Clustering*: group similar data points together (K-Means, DBSCAN)\n",
    "- *Dimensionality reduction*: compress data while preserving structure (PCA, autoencoders)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example scenario:** A retailer has purchase records for 500,000 customers but no predefined segments. K-Means clustering groups them into distinct behavioural profiles (e.g., bargain hunters, brand loyalists, occasional shoppers), enabling targeted marketing campaigns for each segment.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3.3 Reinforcement Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "**Definition:** An **agent** learns to make sequences of decisions by interacting with an **environment**. At each step, the agent observes the current state, takes an action, receives a **reward** signal, and transitions to a new state. The goal is to learn a **policy** that maximises cumulative reward over time.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key components:** Agent · Environment · State · Action · Reward · Policy\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example scenario:** A robot must navigate a warehouse maze to collect items. It has no map and no labelled instructions. Through thousands of trial runs — bumping into walls (negative reward) and successfully reaching destinations (positive reward) — it learns the shortest, safest path."
   ]
  },
