    "### 4.1 Feature Selection\n",
    "\n",
    "Feature selection is the process of identifying which input variables (features) carry the most useful information for predicting the target, while discarding irrelevant or redundant ones.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why it matters:**\n",
    "- Too many features increase model complexity, training time, and the risk of overfitting.\n",
    "- Irrelevant features add noise, degrading predictive performance.\n",
    "- Fewer, well-chosen features improve interpretability and generalization.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Techniques:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Method | Approach | Example |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Filter methods** | Score each feature independently (correlation, mutual information) | Remove features with correlation < 0.05 to the target |\n",
    "| **Wrapper methods** | Evaluate subsets of features using a model (RFE, forward selection) | Recursive Feature Elimination with cross-validation |\n",
    "| **Embedded methods** | Feature selection built into the model's training process | Lasso regression zeroes out unimportant coefficients; tree models provide feature importances |\n",
    "| **Domain knowledge** | Manual selection based on subject-matter expertise | A credit analyst knows that credit history is highly predictive of loan default |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Feature Engineering** extends selection by creating new variables from existing ones — e.g., computing `Debt-to-Income Ratio = Total Debt / Annual Income` from two raw columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4.2 Model Selection\n",
    "\n",
    "Model selection involves choosing the right algorithm family for the problem at hand, balancing accuracy, interpretability, training cost, and production constraints.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key considerations:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Factor | Question to ask |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Problem type | Classification, regression, clustering, or sequential decision-making? |\n",
    "| Dataset size | Small datasets favour simpler models (logistic regression); large datasets unlock deep learning. |\n",
    "| Interpretability | Regulated industries (banking, healthcare) may require explainable models. |\n",
    "| Training time | Real-time retraining favours lightweight models. |\n",
    "| Non-linearity | If the relationship is complex, ensemble methods or neural networks outperform linear models. |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Common algorithm families:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Task | Simpler | More powerful |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Classification | Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes | Random Forest, XGBoost, Neural Network |\n",
    "| Regression | Linear Regression, Ridge | Gradient Boosting, LSTM (time series) |\n",
    "| Clustering | K-Means | DBSCAN, Gaussian Mixture Model |\n",
    "| Dimensionality Reduction | PCA | t-SNE, UMAP, Autoencoder |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Best practice:** Always start with a simple baseline (e.g., logistic regression), measure its performance, then incrementally try more complex models and validate that the added complexity is justified by improved metrics.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4.3 Model Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "Evaluation measures how well the trained model generalises to new, unseen data. It must be performed on data the model has never seen during training.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Evaluation workflow:**\n",
    "1. **Train / Validation / Test split**: Typically 60/20/20 or 70/15/15.\n",
    "2. **Cross-validation** (k-fold): Rotate through k partitions to get a more stable performance estimate and detect overfitting.\n",
    "3. **Choose metrics** appropriate to the task.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Classification metrics:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Definition | Best when… |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Accuracy | % of correct predictions | Classes are balanced |\n",
    "| Precision | Of predicted positives, how many are truly positive? | False positives are costly (e.g., spam filter) |\n",
    "| Recall | Of actual positives, how many did we detect? | False negatives are costly (e.g., disease detection) |\n",
    "| F1-score | Harmonic mean of precision and recall | Imbalanced classes |\n",
    "| ROC-AUC | Discriminative power across all thresholds | Comparing models overall |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Regression metrics:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Definition |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| MAE | Mean Absolute Error — average absolute difference between predicted and actual values |\n",
    "| RMSE | Root Mean Squared Error — penalises large errors more heavily than MAE |\n",
    "| R² | Proportion of variance in the target explained by the model (1 = perfect) |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Overfitting vs Underfitting:**\n",
    "- *Overfitting*: model memorises training data but fails on test data → high train accuracy, low test accuracy. Mitigation: regularisation, more data, simpler model.\n",
    "- *Underfitting*: model is too simple to capture the pattern → low accuracy on both sets. Mitigation: more features, more complex model."
   ]
